# TrustMy.IP (TrustIP) — environment reference
#
# EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS FILE IS OPTIONAL. A deployment with no .env at all —
# or with this whole file blank — is a supported configuration: the backend
# boots, the frontend builds, and every feature whose upstream needs a
# credential is hidden or answers "API key is missing" instead of failing.
# Nothing here is required to run the site.
#
# What you get with zero configuration:
#   working  — IP cards + Query IP (ipinfo.io / ip-api.com / ip.sb, keyless),
#              MaxMind (needs the two .mmdb files on disk — see MAXMIND_*),
#              Whois / RDAP, DNS Resolver, MAC Lookup, IP Calculator, WebRTC,
#              standard DNS-leak test, Speed / Globalping MTR / Latency /
#              Censorship checks, Connectivity check, Service Status, ASN info
#              + history + upstream topology (RIPEstat + CAIDA, keyless),
#              browser fingerprint, security checklist, IP history, achievements,
#              keyboard shortcuts, PWA, dark mode, all shipped languages.
#   hidden   — anything gated on a credential below: Google Static Maps button,
#              Cloudflare Radar views + ASN "live" links, IPCheck.ing API source,
#              shareable report links, per-user features (Firebase), Sentry,
#              Earth Online, in-app docs assistant, curl API.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in only what you want; dotenv reads it at boot, and
# VITE_* values are baked into the frontend bundle at build time (changing one
# needs a rebuild, not just a restart).
#
# Server
# BACKEND_PORT — Express API port (default 11966). frontend-server.js and the
#   Vite dev proxy both read it, so set it once and both halves follow.
# BACKEND_HOST — address the Express API listens on (default 127.0.0.1). The
#   shipped setups (`pnpm start`, pm2, the Docker image) run frontend-server.js
#   on the same host and proxy to 127.0.0.1, so leave it empty there: a client
#   that reaches the API directly skips the proxy that writes X-Forwarded-For.
#   Set 0.0.0.0 (or one interface address) only when the frontend runs on
#   another host or container, and set TRUST_PROXY to that frontend's address.
# FRONTEND_PORT — static server port, the one visitors reach (default 18966).
# ALLOWED_DOMAINS — comma-separated hostnames allowed to call /api/*. The
#   global requireReferer guard rejects any other Origin/Referer with 403.
#   localhost and 127.0.0.1 are always allowed, so an empty value only breaks
#   non-localhost deployments: on a real domain, set this or every API call
#   from a visitor gets 403.
BACKEND_PORT="11966"
BACKEND_HOST=""
FRONTEND_PORT="18966"
ALLOWED_DOMAINS=""

# SITE
# VITE_SITE_URL — the single source of this deployment's canonical origin.
#   It feeds the canonical / og:url / twitter:image / JSON-LD block in
#   index.html (substituted by siteUrlHtmlPlugin in vite.config.js) and the
#   third segment of the outbound User-Agent (common/upstream-ua.js).
#   Unset: that whole conditional <head> block is dropped from the build (no
#   `undefined` is ever emitted) and the UA degrades to `TrustIP/v<version>`.
#   Nothing stops working — you only lose canonical-tag/og absolute URLs, so
#   set it to your public origin as soon as you have one.
VITE_SITE_URL=""

# THIRD-PARTY APIs — each key enables one data source; empty keeps it off.
# GOOGLE_MAP_API_KEY — the Static Maps image behind the IP card's map button.
#   Empty: the button is hidden (configs.map=false); nothing else changes.
GOOGLE_MAP_API_KEY=""
# IPINFO_API_KEY — raises ipinfo.io from its keyless free quota to an
#   authenticated one. Empty still works and the source card stays listed: the
#   handler calls ipinfo.io without a token.
#   (IPINFO_API_TOKEN is the pre-rename spelling, still read as a fallback.)
IPINFO_API_KEY=""
# IPAPIIS_API_KEY — api.ipapi.is geolocation + isHosting / isProxy flags.
#   Comma-separated keys are rotated at random. Empty: /api/ipapiis reads the
#   keyless free tier (~1,000 lookups a day per server) — location and AS only,
#   no privacy flags — and the source card stays listed.
IPAPIIS_API_KEY=""
# TRUST_IPAPIIS_FEED=true — also let the trust score and the dossier ask
#   api.ipapi.is about every *queried* address, not only the visitor's own, which
#   fills the proxy / VPN, Tor-exit and abuse-history signals. Off by default: the
#   geolocation card spends this key's quota one call per visitor, and a per-lookup
#   feed multiplies it by whatever an audit script polls — an exhausted key shows
#   up as a data gap, not as an outage. Any other value: those three signals stay
#   declared gaps, and are left out of the confidence denominator rather than
#   counted against a deployment that never claimed the subscription.
#   Requires IPAPIIS_API_KEY.
TRUST_IPAPIIS_FEED=""
# IP2LOCATION_API_KEY — api.ip2location.io geolocation. Empty: the handler asks
#   the same endpoint keyless, which the vendor serves at 1,000 lookups a day per
#   server with the same body; a key lifts that to the plan's quota.
IP2LOCATION_API_KEY=""
# PROXYCHECK_API_KEY — proxycheck.io's anonymiser list (1,000 queries a day on the
#   free plan). Comma-separated keys are rotated at random. Empty: the provider
#   reports `unavailable` and adds no vote; the upstream does answer keyless
#   queries, but on one shared anonymous budget, which somebody else's traffic can
#   exhaust — so a per-lookup feed is asked only where a key says whose quota it is.
PROXYCHECK_API_KEY=""
# ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY — abuseipdb.com's abuse census (90-day window, key mandatory:
#   the upstream has no keyless mode). Comma-separated keys are rotated at random.
#   Empty: the provider reports `unavailable` and the abuse signal stays a declared
#   gap rather than a measurement this instance never made.
ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY=""
# TRUST_PROVIDERS — which of those vendors the trust score and the dossier ask
#   about every *queried* address, comma-separated (`proxycheck`, `abuseipdb`,
#   `ip2location`, `ipinfo`, `internetdb`, `locallists`; the dotted `proxycheck.io` spelling works
#   too). Off by default for the same arithmetic as TRUST_IPAPIIS_FEED: these are
#   per-lookup calls on keys other routes spend per visitor, so a deployment opts in
#   having decided what its quota is for. A listed vendor whose key is absent is not
#   a failure — it reports `unavailable`, and the response is still a 200 with the
#   key-free sources in it. `internetdb` (Shodan's scan archive) needs no key:
#   listing it is a decision about consulting a third party's scan history on every
#   lookup, and it is what opens the dossier's open-ports row. `ipinfo` is listed
#   only where the account has IPinfo's paid Privacy Detection: the free Lite body
#   carries no `privacy` object, so that vendor answers `ok` while casting no vote —
#   it would appear among the consulted sources without adding any evidence. The key
#   still earns its keep as a geolocation card (`/api/ipinfo`), which is a separate
#   question from this list. `locallists` needs no key and asks nothing: it lets the
#   downloaded Tor exit / X4BNet lists (ANONYMITY_LISTS) vote in the score's Tor and
#   proxy / VPN rows, which the dossier otherwise only displays.
TRUST_PROVIDERS=""
# TRUST_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS — how long a multi-source answer (the keyed abuse feed and
#   the vendor lists) is kept for one address, shared through Workers KV when the
#   CLOUDFLARE_* trio below is set and through this process' cache when it is not.
#   Default 86400 (a day), because the vendors re-evaluate their lists on roughly a
#   daily cycle; clamped to [60s, 7d] so a mistyped value costs freshness or quota
#   rather than silently disabling either. Empty or non-numeric is the default.
#   Registry facts (RIPEstat, RIR, PTR) keep their own 6-hour clock, and blocklist
#   membership its own 30 minutes — a listing moves within days and must not be held
#   on a subscription's schedule.
TRUST_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS="86400"
# TRUST_PROVIDER_DAILY_MAX — how many lookups per vendor per UTC day this deployment
#   is willing to spend, counted per process. 0 (default) means no cap. Past it a
#   vendor answers `unavailable` with reason `quota` and asks nothing, so an audit
#   script walking a /8 cannot spend the same key the geolocation cards rely on.
#   The ceiling is yours to state, not a number copied from a pricing page: free
#   tiers change, and a baked-in one throttles a deployment for a quota it never had.
#   Two instances each count to the ceiling, so multiply by your process count.
TRUST_PROVIDER_DAILY_MAX="0"
# GITHUB_TOKEN — optional GitHub token for the nav's star badge
#   (/api/github-stars): sent as `Authorization: Bearer`, lifting GitHub's
#   60-per-hour per-IP anonymous limit to 5,000 per hour. Empty = anonymous.
#   Either way the last count GitHub returned is kept in
#   common/github-stars-db/last-good.json and served (`stale: true`) when GitHub
#   fails, so a rate-limited hour shows the last real number, never a fake 0.
#   （zh-TW）申請：https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new
#   →「Fine-grained token」→ Repository access 選「Public repositories (read-only)」，
#   不需要勾任何權限；或 classic token（https://github.com/settings/tokens）不勾任何 scope。
#   免費；請設定到期日並定期更新。
GITHUB_TOKEN=""
# GLOBALPING_TOKEN — optional Globalping API token. Set, the MTR / latency /
#   censorship tools and the dossier's latency grid create measurements through
#   POST /api/globalping/measurements, which adds `Authorization: Bearer` (polls
#   still go browser → api.globalping.io; results need no token), and
#   /api/configs reports globalpingProxy=true. Empty = every visitor's browser
#   creates measurements anonymously on its own per-IP quota (the default). The
#   token's quota is shared by every visitor of this deployment, so set it on a
#   personal or low-traffic instance; the route keeps a 10-per-minute per-IP
#   limit. Either way a 429 pauses all tools on the page for its Retry-After and
#   says so, instead of failing each run.
#   （zh-TW）申請：https://dash.globalping.io/ 用 GitHub 帳號登入 → Tokens →
#   Generate new token（免費）。匿名每個 IP 每小時約 250 次測量；登入後的
#   token 每小時約 500 次，另可透過贊助或掛 probe 取得額外 credits（以官方
#   https://globalping.io/docs/api.globalping.io 的 Rate limits 說明為準）。
GLOBALPING_TOKEN=""
# IP_CARD_FONT_PATH — the font the shareable IP card (`GET /api/ip-card`) draws with:
#   a .ttf/.otf file, or a directory to pick one from. Unset, the well-known system
#   locations are probed (DejaVu / Noto / Liberation on Linux, the macOS and Windows
#   font folders). When nothing is readable the route answers 503 before asking any
#   registry and /api/configs reports `ipCard: false`, so the UI hides the share
#   button — a container with no font package installed is a supported deployment.
#   Coverage is your choice too: the default faces carry Latin, Cyrillic and Greek and
#   not Han, so point this at a CJK face if the card is offered in Chinese.
IP_CARD_FONT_PATH=""
# (MAC Lookup needs no key: it answers from the IEEE registries, downloaded as
#   the `oui` offline dataset below, so MAC_LOOKUP_API_KEY is no longer read.)
# RIPESTAT_SOURCE_APP — the `sourceapp` identifier sent to RIPEstat with every
#   as-overview / routing-history call; empty sends `trustip`. Set it only to
#   register your own deployment name with RIPE NCC.
RIPESTAT_SOURCE_APP=""
# DNSBL_RESOLVERS — comma-separated recursive resolvers queried directly for
#   blocklist (/api/dnsbl, and the trust score's blocklists signal). Empty uses
#   8.8.8.8,1.1.1.1. Each candidate is proved honest by a canary name before any
#   listing is reported; a resolver that answers a name that cannot exist is
#   skipped and the next one tried. Point it at your own recursor when the
#   public ones are intercepted on your network — with no truthful path the
#   signal reports unmeasured, never clean.
DNSBL_RESOLVERS=""
# CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY — Cloudflare Radar (traffic / BGP / quality / outage
#   feeds behind /api/cfradar) and the ASN "live in Radar" links.
#   Empty: those views answer "API key is missing" and the Radar panels are
#   hidden. Also needs the "Workers KV Storage: Edit" permission when used for
#   shareable reports below. (CLOUDFLARE_API is the pre-rename fallback.)
CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY=""

# Shareable diagnostic reports — stored in Cloudflare Workers KV. All three
# CLOUDFLARE_* values must be set (CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY additionally needs the
# "Workers KV Storage: Edit" permission) or POST/GET /api/report answer 503 and
# /api/configs reports reportSharing=false, so the "Share" dialog stays
# hidden. CLOUDFLARE_KV_NAMESPACE_ID is the namespace's hex ID from the
# dashboard, not its name.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=""
CLOUDFLARE_KV_NAMESPACE_ID=""

# IPCHECKING_API_KEY + IPCHECKING_API_ENDPOINT — the upstream project's
#   private IPCheck.ing API: the IPCheck.ing IP card, the enhanced DNS-leak
#   test, the invisibility test, Persona Check, achievements/user info.
#   BOTH are needed — a key without an endpoint is treated as unconfigured.
#   This fork has no credentials for it, so leave both empty: /api/configs
#   reports ipChecking=false, the gated tools stay hidden, and the browser
#   detects the visitor's own IP through the public trace endpoints instead.
IPCHECKING_API_KEY=""
IPCHECKING_API_ENDPOINT=""

# Offline datasets — read locally, refreshed by one updater engine
# (common/dataset-updater.js, rows in common/datasets.js). The backend listens
# first and downloads whatever is missing in the background; a route whose data
# is still on its way answers 503 "Offline data is loading" until it lands.
#   maxmind    GeoLite2 City + ASN in common/maxmind-db/ — downloaded only when
#              MAXMIND_ACCOUNT_ID + MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY are both set (free
#              license key from maxmind.com). Or drop GeoLite2-City.mmdb +
#              GeoLite2-ASN.mmdb into that folder yourself. GeoLite2 is always
#              preferred when its two files are there.
#   dbip       DB-IP IP-to-City Lite + IP-to-ASN Lite (CC BY 4.0, monthly) in
#              common/dbip-db/ — the keyless stand-in, downloaded only when
#              the MaxMind credentials are absent (~70 MB gzip, ~150 MB on
#              disk). While it serves, /api/maxmind answers carry
#              `source: "dbip-lite"` + `attribution`, and the footer shows
#              "IP Geolocation by DB-IP" (the licence's attribution duty).
#              With neither database the backend still boots and /api/maxmind
#              answers 503 "MaxMind database is not ready".
#   as2org /   CAIDA AS-to-organization and AS-relationship snapshots in
#   as-rel     common/as-org-db/ and common/as-rel-db/ — no credentials exist
#              for them, so they bootstrap at boot with zero configuration.
#   peeringdb  PeeringDB dump (via CAIDA), for the ASN Profile — only fetched
#              when CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY is set (a ~116 MB daily download). Without
#              it the ASN Profile's peering section and the dossier's ASN type /
#              traffic rows ask the PeeringDB public API per ASN instead
#              (PEERINGDB_API below), cached for a day.
#   oui        IEEE MAC registries behind MAC Lookup — keyless, always fetched.
#   tor-exit   Tor Project bulk exit list (check.torproject.org) in
#              common/tor-exit-db/ — keyless, the dossier's Tor row.
#   x4bnet     X4BNet VPN + datacenter IPv4 CIDR lists (github.com/X4BNet/
#              lists_vpn, MIT) in common/x4bnet-db/ — keyless, the dossier's
#              VPN row and hosting-list tag. Both anonymity rows refuse a
#              download outside their size caps (common/anonymity-lists.js).
#              They are shown on the dossier, never scored, unless
#              TRUST_PROVIDERS lists `locallists`.
# All of these directories are gitignored (non-commercial / third-party terms).
# （zh-TW）MaxMind GeoLite2 金鑰申請：https://www.maxmind.com/en/geolite2/signup
#   註冊免費帳號 → 登入 → Account → Manage License Keys → Generate new license key；
#   MAXMIND_ACCOUNT_ID 是帳號頁上的 Account ID。免費版需遵守 GeoLite2 EULA，
#   每日下載次數有限（官方說明每 24 小時 30 次）。未設定時改用免金鑰的 DB-IP Lite。
MAXMIND_ACCOUNT_ID=""
MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY=""
# DBIP_LITE — "false" skips the DB-IP Lite fallback (then /api/maxmind stays 503
#   without GeoLite2). Empty = on whenever the MaxMind credentials are absent.
#   （zh-TW）DB-IP Lite 免金鑰、免申請；授權 CC BY 4.0，必須在顯示資料處標示
#   「IP Geolocation by DB-IP」並連結 https://db-ip.com（頁尾已自動處理）。
DBIP_LITE=""
# ANONYMITY_LISTS — "false" skips the tor-exit and x4bnet rows entirely (no
#   download, the dossier's Tor / VPN rows read "no data"). Empty = on.
#   （zh-TW）Tor 與 X4BNet 清單皆免金鑰、免申請；設為 "false" 才會停用。
ANONYMITY_LISTS=""
# PEERINGDB_API — "false" turns off the per-ASN PeeringDB API fallback (no
#   request leaves the server; with no local index those rows read "not
#   measured"). Empty = on. Anonymous use works, throttled per server IP; a 429
#   pauses every lookup for its Retry-After instead of retrying per visitor.
# PEERINGDB_API_KEY — optional PeeringDB API key, sent as
#   `Authorization: Api-Key <key>`; lifts the anonymous throttle. Empty =
#   anonymous.
#   （zh-TW）申請：登入 https://www.peeringdb.com/ → 右上角帳號 → Profile →
#   「API Keys」→ Add（免費；需先註冊帳號並驗證 e-mail）。只讀公開資料即可，
#   不需要申請任何組織權限。
PEERINGDB_API=""
PEERINGDB_API_KEY=""
# DATASET_AUTO_UPDATE — the periodic re-check, at DATASET_UPDATE_CRON
#   (server-local time). Missing datasets are always downloaded at boot either
#   way; this only decides whether the ones on disk are refreshed later.
#   "false" leaves them alone (this fork's default: the snapshot you booted with
#   ages but never surprises a request path); "true" re-checks every row daily
#   (a fresh as-rel2 takes ~1 min and a few hundred MB of scratch space).
#   Empty: the pre-engine flags MAXMIND_AUTO_UPDATE / CAIDA_AUTO_UPDATE decide
#   per dataset when set, and otherwise the re-check is ON.
# DATASET_UPDATE_CRON — five- or six-field cron pattern for that re-check
#   (default "30 4 * * *", 04:30). A pattern croner rejects logs a ⚠️ warning
#   and turns the re-check off; the server and the datasets on disk keep running.
DATASET_AUTO_UPDATE="false"
DATASET_UPDATE_CRON="30 4 * * *"

# User system (Firebase Auth) — all three are required to enable sign-in, and
# sign-in is only useful if IPCHECKING_API_KEY above is set too: the user's
# profile, achievements and enhanced DNS-leak sessions live behind that API.
# With any of them empty, firebase/app and firebase/auth never enter the
# bundle (lazy, env-gated import), the nav shows the standalone preferences
# cog instead of a user menu, and the browser logs no Firebase errors.
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=""
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=""
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=""

# VITE_INVISIBILITY_TEST_KEY — the proxy-detection script key for the
# Invisibility Test, issued by the IPCheck.ing service that runs the check.
# Like IPCHECKING_API_KEY this belongs to the upstream service: without it the
# tool is hidden outright (it is gated on the canonical deployment), so a
# self-hosted fork never loads the third-party script.
VITE_INVISIBILITY_TEST_KEY=""

# DOCS SITE — origin of the GitBook docs site powering the in-app
# assistant and the Help Center link. Empty disables both.
VITE_DOCS_URL=""

# EARTH ONLINE — full URL of the pulse backend (trailing slashes stripped).
# Empty hides the social parts (status feed / visitor map / visit beacon) at
# build time, like Sentry. The outage feed is independent — it only needs
# CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY above.
VITE_PULSE_BEACON_URL=""

# CURL API — the hostnames printed in the "get your IP from the terminal"
# card. They must be domains you serve yourself; all three must be set or the
# card is hidden (store.curlDomainsHadSet). Empty: nothing breaks, the card
# simply does not render.
VITE_CURL_IPV4_DOMAIN=""
VITE_CURL_IPV6_DOMAIN=""
VITE_CURL_IPV64_DOMAIN=""

# TEST-ONLY — extra STUN servers (comma-separated host:port, e.g.
# "127.0.0.1:3478,127.0.0.1:3479") APPENDED to the WebRTC test-process log's probe
# matrix (utils/webrtc-ice.js probeServers). For proving the log on a machine with
# no outbound UDP against a STUN server you run locally; malformed entries are
# dropped. Leave empty in production: empty means exactly the public servers.
VITE_TEST_EXTRA_STUN=""

# Google Analytics — a GA4 measurement id (G-XXXX…). Empty means gtag.js is
# never requested and the privacy page states no analytics notice. Setting it
# starts sending page views to Google, so it is off by default on purpose.
VITE_GOOGLE_ANALYTICS_ID=""

# Security related
# SECURITY_BLACKLIST_LOG_FILE_PATH: opt-in on-disk ledger of rate-limited IPs
# (e.g. "logs/blacklist-ip.log"). Empty = no file; the event is always logged
# via the shared logger either way (and mirrored to Sentry when configured).
SECURITY_BLACKLIST_LOG_FILE_PATH=""
# SECURITY_RATE_LIMIT — requests per 20 minutes per IP before a 429. 0 / empty
# = no limiter (the default). Only worth setting on an internet-facing box.
# SECURITY_DELAY_AFTER — after this many requests in an hour each response is
# delayed by (count − limit) × 400 ms, capped at 5 s. 0 / empty = off.
SECURITY_RATE_LIMIT=""
SECURITY_DELAY_AFTER=""
# TRUST_PROXY — which socket peers may name the client in X-Forwarded-For
#   (Express `trust proxy`). Every per-IP limiter, the ledger above and the
#   X-Forwarded-For sent to IPCheck.ing use the address this resolves. Empty =
#   `loopback`: right for `pnpm start`, pm2 and the Docker image, where
#   frontend-server.js proxies to the backend on the same host and overwrites
#   the header with the visitor's socket address; a client reaching the backend
#   directly cannot choose its address. Also accepts `false`, a hop count, or a
#   comma list of `loopback` / `linklocal` / `uniquelocal` / addresses / CIDRs
#   (a reverse proxy on another host). `true` is refused (it believes any
#   client) and falls back to `loopback` with a warning. Getting it wrong
#   breaks no feature: too narrow puts every visitor in one limiter bucket,
#   too wide lets clients pick their own.
# TRUST_CF_CONNECTING_IP — set to 1 only when Cloudflare fronts the site (and
#   the origin accepts connections from Cloudflare alone). Then CF-Connecting-IP
#   is the client address for the limiters, the ledger, upstream headers and the
#   Sentry tunnel's `user.ip_address`. Empty = the header is ignored, because
#   anywhere else a client writes it itself; behind Cloudflare without it, every
#   visitor shares the edge's address. X-Real-IP is never read.
TRUST_PROXY=""
TRUST_CF_CONNECTING_IP=""
# SITE_RATE_LIMIT — the edge function's own ceiling (functions/), because the
#   Express middleware above does not run there and a hosted deployment is the one
#   place an anonymous visitor drives a third party's free quota directly.
#   Requests per 15-minute window per client address; empty or non-numeric is the
#   default 600, 0 turns it off. The client is the platform-written
#   CF-Connecting-IP only (X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP are client-writable and
#   ignored); a request without it shares one `unknown` bucket rather than going
#   unlimited. At most 10,000 addresses are tracked, least recently seen evicted. It counts per warm isolate, not per deployment:
#   a cold start begins clean and N instances each spend the ceiling, so this is a
#   brake on one script rather than a distributed firewall. Set it to 0 when your
#   gateway already throttles, so a second blind limit is not stacked on top.
SITE_RATE_LIMIT=""
# CSP_ENFORCE — frontend-server.js sends a Content-Security-Policy built in
#   common/csp.js: the inline scripts of dist/index.html (hashed at startup, so
#   restart after a rebuild), Google Analytics, Firebase sign-in, the invisibility
#   probe, and VITE_DOCS_URL / VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN when set (read here at
#   runtime, so keep them the same as at build time). Empty = it goes out as
#   Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: violations only show in the browser
#   console (or at CSP_REPORT_URI) and nothing breaks. 1 = enforced; a feature
#   whose origin the policy misses then stops working instead of reporting.
# CSP_REPORT_URI — optional endpoint for the browser's violation reports
#   (`report-uri`). Empty = no reports are sent anywhere.
CSP_ENFORCE=""
CSP_REPORT_URI=""

# Sentry — all optional; leave empty and no Sentry code ships or runs at all.
# VITE_SENTRY_DSN_FRONTEND: frontend DSN (build-time; baked into the bundle;
#   also read by the backend at runtime as the allowlist for /api/monitoring,
#   the first-party tunnel that relays envelopes past ad blockers). Without it
#   the tunnel route is not even mounted.
# SENTRY_DSN_BACKEND: backend DSN (runtime; read by sentry-instrument.js)
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT: backend environment tag — unset means "production",
#   set "development" on dev machines (frontend tags itself automatically)
# SENTRY_ORG / SENTRY_PROJECT_FRONTEND / SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN: source-map upload
# at build time only — the token is a secret, never exposed to the browser
VITE_SENTRY_DSN_FRONTEND=""
SENTRY_DSN_BACKEND=""
SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=""
SENTRY_ORG=""
SENTRY_PROJECT_FRONTEND=""
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=""

